---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="ounces-to-grams">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="ounces-to-grams" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The number to remember is 28.35</h2>
    <p>
      To convert ounces to grams, use 28.349523125: one avoirdupois ounce in grams. 28.35 is safe for
      cooking and post. 28 gives a 1.23% error, which is a whole gram at 3 oz and five grams at a
      pound.
    </p>
    <p>
      The exact value comes from the pound: 0.45359237 kg by definition, divided by sixteen. All figures
      on this page use it, so extra decimal places are real digits, not noise.
    </p>

    <h2>The quarter-pound ladder</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Ounces</th><th scope="col">Grams</th><th scope="col">Also known as</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>28.35</td><td>A nutrition-label portion of cheese or nuts</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>56.70</td><td>An eighth of a pound</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>113.40</td><td>A quarter pound. One US stick of butter</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8</td><td>226.80</td><td>Half a pound</td></tr>
        <tr><td>12</td><td>340.19</td><td>Three quarters of a pound. A standard US can</td></tr>
        <tr><td>16</td><td>453.59</td><td>One pound</td></tr>
        <tr><td>32</td><td>907.18</td><td>Two pounds</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Fractions are the normal input</h2>
    <p>
      Older British recipes use halves and quarters of an ounce, and jewellery and spice work uses
      eighths. Type <code>1/2</code>, <code>3/4</code>, <code>1 1/2</code> or <code>2&frac14;</code>,
      and the tool reads them directly, so you do not need to convert the fraction to a decimal first.
    </p>

    <h2>Ounces of liquid do not convert here</h2>
    <p>
      If your recipe says "8 oz of milk", it almost certainly means eight fluid ounces (a volume), and
      this page gives the wrong answer. Eight US fluid ounces of milk is 237 mL, which weighs about
      244 g, not the 227 g you get when you treat it as weight. In US recipes, liquids are fluid ounces
      and solids are weight ounces. In British and professional recipes, almost everything is weight.
    </p>

    <h2>Troy ounces for metals</h2>
    <p>
      Gold, silver, platinum and palladium are priced per troy ounce, which is 31.1034768 g. A one troy
      ounce bar is about 10% heavier than one avoirdupois ounce, and the wrong figure changes a bullion
      valuation by about a tenth. Twelve troy ounces make a troy pound (373.24 g), not sixteen.
    </p>

    <h2>Grams are better for cooking</h2>
    <p>
      A gram is small enough that you rarely need a fraction, which removes a whole class of error.
      When a recipe is converted, write the gram figures on the page and stop converting. Each round
      trip through a rounded ounce figure loses a little more precision.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
